namespace FlexFlow {
/**

\page substitutions substitutions

\brief Contains the definitions of PCG substitutions (i.e., \ref Substitution), as well as the code for serializing them.

\section substitutions-usage Usage

\subsection substitutions-creating Creating a Substitution

To create a \ref Substitution, you'll want to use the \ref SubstitutionBuilder,
which provides a more concise interface for substitution construction, while
also preventing some common errors. Here is an example of using \ref SubstitutionBuilder
to build a simple \ref "Substitution":

\snippet lib/substitutions/test/src/substitutions/substitution_builder.cc SubstitutionBuilder example

In practice the \ref SubstitutionBuilder interface can still end up being
excessively verbose due to its generality.
In many cases, however, it is possible to identify some common patterns of your
substitutions and pull them out as helper functions, which can make creating substitutions
considerably more precise. For an example of this pattern, see the following
code used in constructing the unity substitution set.

\snippet lib/substitutions/src/substitutions/unity_substitution_set.cc SubstitutionBuilder more concise example

\subsection substitutions-applying Applying a Substitution

To apply a \ref Substitution to a \ref ParallelComputationGraph, you'll first need to identify a location in the
\ref ParallelComputationGraph where it can be applied.
For this you'll use the \ref find_pattern_matches function, which returns a
\c std::vector of \ref PCGPatternMatch, each of which represents one of those valid locations to apply the substitution.
Once you've chosen a \ref PCGPatternMatch to use, you can then use \ref apply_substitution to create the new PCG.
However, for full generality this function returns a \ref SubParallelComputationGraph rather than a \ref ParallelComputationGraph.
Fortunately, it is easy to convert between these two using the \ref
pcg_from_sub_pcg_by_dropping_inputs and \ref sub_pcg_from_full_pcg functions.
For a concrete example of applying a \ref Substitution, see the code snippet below:

\snippet lib/compiler/src/compiler/unity_algorithm/unity_algorithm.cc apply_substitution example

\section substitutions-internals Internals

\subsection substitution Substitution

A \ref Substitution is to replace a subgraph of the PCG by a new one. We refer
to the subgraph to be replaced as the input graph, and the new subgraph to
replace the input graph as the output graph.

\subsection pattern-matches PCGPattern and MultiDiGraphPatternMatch

A \ref PCGPattern is defined as an open graph with node label OperatorPattern
and output label ParallelTensorPattern, which is refered to as the pattern
graph. The graph structure of a GraphPattern instance defines the geometrical
property of the input graph, while the node labels and output labels define the
attribute property of that.

To apply a substitution to a PCG, we should first match the pattern graph to a subgraph of the PCG. `MultiDiGraphPatternMatch` describes the match, which consists of
* `node_assignment`: a mapping from the nodes of the pattern graph to the nodes of the PCG; and
* `edge_assignment`: a mapping from the edges of the pattern graph to the nodes of the PCG.
The input graph derived by this match is then defined by `values(node_assignment)` and `values(edge_assignment)`. A match is valid if and only if
* `node_assignment` and `edge_assignment` are injections;
* For every node `n` in the pattern graph, `edge_assignment` derives a bijection between `query_edges({n})` and `query_edges({node_assignment.at_l(n)})`.

\subsection output-graph-expr OutputGraphExpr

An \ref OutputGraphExpr is defined as an open graph with node label \ref
OutputOperatorAttrsAssignment and output label \c std::monostate.

\ref OutputOperatorAttrsAssignment is a collection of \ref OperatorAttributeKey
and \ref OutputOperatorAttributeExpr pairs. It defines how the attributes of a
single operator is calculated from the input graph. A pair \c
"{operator_attribute_key, output_operator_attribute_expr}" in the collection
means the value of \c output_operator_attribute_expr is assigned to the
attribute named \c operator_attribute_key of the operator.



*/
}
